 ### [3D Ellipsoid Projection](15723-3d-ellipsoid-projection.md)
 3D Ellipsoid Projection is a Gaussian selection method used to identify candidate 3D Gaussians for rendering specific image slices in volume rendering pipelines. It approximates the spatial extent of a 3D Gaussian ellipsoid as a sphere to compute a uniform 2D bounding box, which is then projected orthogonally onto every image slice. Unlike sibling methods that adapt bounding boxes per slice or use complex covariance corrections, this approach prioritizes simplicity by treating the Gaussian's range as isotropic, though this leads to redundant computation for off-center slices.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(M)$ for projection of $M$ Gaussians, where each projection involves constant-time matrix operations ($3 \times 3$ and $2 \times 2$). Sorting takes $O(M \log M)$. Rasterization is $O(P)$ where $P$ is the number of pixels, but optimized by tile-based binning to only process relevant splats.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ to store the projected splats.
-*   **Measured Performance:** The method enables real-time rendering of millions of Gaussians. For example, on the Mip-NeRF360 benchmark, 3DGS reaches 27.21 dB PSNR and 0.214 LPIPS at 134 fps [S3, unofficial]. An accelerator implementation achieved 129 FPS at Full HD (1080p) [S12].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with the number of Gaussians. Performance is heavily dependent on the efficiency of the sorting and tile-based rasterization stages.
-*   **Strengths:** Closed-form projection allows for differentiable rendering, enabling gradient-based optimization of scene parameters. High rendering speed due to efficient rasterization.
-*   **Limitations:** The affine approximation of perspective projection can introduce artifacts for very large Gaussians or extreme viewpoints. Requires careful handling of numerical stability (covariance stabilization).
-*   **When to use:** When real-time or differentiable rendering of large-scale 3D scenes is required.
-*   **When not to use:** When high geometric accuracy for very large primitives is critical, or when memory bandwidth is severely constrained and $M$ is extremely large.
